1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,350
How do we stop Architectural
Intent being lost as

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:08,520
software is built? Hello,
I'm Russell and welcome to

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:11,960
the Articulate Journal where
I'm exploring what it takes

4
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:15,960
to build an AI-native architecture
from first principles through

5
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:18,920
the design and implementation
of a reference architecture for

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,160
Architectural Intelligence. This
isn't a tutorial series or a

7
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:24,980
product demo. It's a public
engineering journal documenting the

8
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:28,985
architectural decisions experiments
and lessons learned as the architecture

9
00:00:29,065 --> 00:00:36,150
evolves. In the last two
episodes, we built the

10
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:41,530
conceptual foundation for Articulate.
We introduced the Knowledge Model

11
00:00:41,610 --> 00:00:45,770
as the place where architectural
understanding lives.

12
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:49,200
We also explored how AI
can retrieve and reason

13
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,530
over that knowledge. At
this point, it feels like

14
00:00:52,610 --> 00:00:56,070
we've got a good
understanding of this part of

15
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:59,280
the architecture. But there's
something important missing.

16
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,380
Understanding an architecture
isn't the same as understanding how

17
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:06,450
it's intended to behave.
That raises another question.

18
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:11,340
How do we preserve
architectural intent as an idea

19
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:15,440
it becomes running software?
Let's imagine we've just finished

20
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:19,190
designing a new capability. We've
had conversations with domain

21
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:22,730
experts. We've captured
claims in the Knowledge Model.

22
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:26,800
We've reasoned about constraints.
We've agreed on the behaviour.

23
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:32,220
Everything feels clear. At that
moment, the architecture exists

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,810
almost perfectly. But only
in our current understanding.

25
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:37,820
Now we have to build it.

26
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:39,790
What we really want is
surprisingly simple.

27
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:43,130
We want the capability
to keep its meaning as

28
00:01:43,170 --> 00:01:45,790
it moves through the implementation.

29
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:48,570
We don't want to lose why it exists or

30
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:51,960
outcomes it should produce.
The rules it must obey

31
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,450
and the policies it operates
under and the behaviours

32
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:59,050
that we expect. Ideally, those
things should remain true

33
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:01,680
regardless of how we eventually
implement the system.

34
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:05,810
That might sound obvious,
but it rarely happens.

35
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:08,840
This is where architecture begins to leak.

36
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,810
The capability starts life
as an architectural idea.

37
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,420
Then it becomes a requirement.

38
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:16,870
The requirement becomes backlog items.

39
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:20,140
The backlog items become
source code and source code

40
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:24,920
becomes deployments. In an AI-native
system, behaviour may disappear

41
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:28,800
even further. Some intent
ends up inside prompts.

42
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:32,440
Some becomes workflow definitions.
Some is hidden in validation

43
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:35,670
logic and some is scattered
across configuration.

44
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:38,790
Some survives only in old documents.

45
00:02:39,090 --> 00:02:42,310
By the time the software
reaches production, the original

46
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:44,840
architectural intent has been fragmented.

47
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:48,370
The software may work
perfectly, but recovering why it

48
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:52,830
behaves that way does become
increasingly difficult.

49
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,250
This isn't simply a documentation problem.

50
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:01,730
It's a representation problem. While
exploring AI-native architecture, I

51
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,680
kept running into this problem.

52
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:07,040
The Knowledge Model was excellent
for representing what we

53
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:10,550
understood about the architecture,
but it wasn't the right

54
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:13,820
place to describe how a
capability should behave.

55
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:15,450
That led me to a different question.

56
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:21,790
What if capability behaviour became
a first-class architectural concept?

57
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:26,670
Not services, APIs, agents
or workflows, capabilities.

58
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:33,970
Capabilities that describe outcomes,
behaviour, rules, policies, invariants,

59
00:03:34,010 --> 00:03:39,900
effects and life cycle. Without
saying anything about implementation,

60
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:43,967
that idea became the foundation
of DCL, the Declarative

61
00:03:44,047 --> 00:03:48,640
Capability Language. DCL isn't
intended to replace source code,

62
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,610
and it doesn't replace documentation,
and it certainly doesn't

63
00:03:52,690 --> 00:03:56,570
replace prompts. Instead, it
provides a durable description of

64
00:03:56,620 --> 00:04:00,810
architectural intent, one
that both humans and AI can

65
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:06,820
understand consistently. This
also clarified something else.

66
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:11,790
The Knowledge Model and DCL
have different responsibilities.

67
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:15,920
The Knowledge Model represents what
Articulate currently understands about

68
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,090
an architecture. It can contain
uncertainty, conflicting claims,

69
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:24,780
incomplete evidence and knowledge evolves.

70
00:04:25,070 --> 00:04:29,670
DCL is different. DCL represents
behaviour once the understanding

71
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:34,480
has become sufficiently clear. The
progression isn't conversation to

72
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:39,800
DCL. Instead, it becomes conversation,
knowledge, understanding, DCL.

73
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:43,830
In this context, the language
isn't the starting point.

74
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:47,790
It's the result of
architectural understanding.

75
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:50,860
This changes how I think
about architecture.

76
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:53,900
A capability is not an API or a service,

77
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,790
an agent or a workflow.

78
00:04:55,980 --> 00:05:00,200
Those are implementation choices.
The capability should survive, even

79
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:02,680
if every one of those
technologies changes.

80
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,780
Today, a capability might be
implemented by deterministic software.

81
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:09,820
Tomorrow, it might use an agent.

82
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:12,900
Next year, it could become
a distributed workflow.

83
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:16,800
The implementation evolves,
but the capability remains.

84
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:20,140
That's why capabilities
feel like much more of a

85
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:24,990
durable architectural building
block. This also changes how I

86
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:30,190
think about AI-native systems.
It's easy to describe an

87
00:05:30,270 --> 00:05:34,080
architecture using agents, whether
you've got a research agent,

88
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:35,570
a planning agent, a decision
agent or a knowledge

89
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:37,420
agent. But agents are actors.

90
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:41,900
They perform work. They aren't the
architectural responsibility itself.

91
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:47,590
The capability is. Once I
realized that, the architecture

92
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:51,810
became much clearer. The runtime
exists to execute capabilities,

93
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:53,750
not the other way round.

94
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:59,810
At this point, Articulate has three
complementary architectural assets.

95
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:02,860
The conversation, which
discovers knowledge.

96
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:08,340
The Knowledge Model, which captures
architectural understanding, and DCL

97
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,550
formalizes capability behaviour.
Each solves a different problem.

98
00:06:12,850 --> 00:06:16,960
Together, they preserve architectural
intent from discovery through to

99
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:20,760
implementation. And that's
exactly what we'll need as we

100
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:25,280
move into implementation.
Because now, we know what we

101
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:30,730
understand, how we reason, and
how capabilities should behave.

102
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:33,840
we can finally ask a different question.

103
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:37,790
What kind of runtime do those
capabilities actually need?

104
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,300
That becomes the first major
architectural decision of the

105
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:44,180
implementation journey. And
that is the topic for the

106
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:47,660
next episode. Selecting an agent runtime.
